"# Task: Conformance Review of `DeleteSpaceMember`\n\nReview the implementation and all directly relevant call paths for `DeleteSpaceMember` in the specified Memos revision. Determine its actual authorization behavior and compare that behavior with the independently supplied policy below.\n\n## Repository scope\n\n- **Repository:** `usememos/memos`\n- **Revision:** `cb42e326ba9cc266a6a9570c53e0fe04c62793f4`\n- **Allowed source paths only:**\n  - `server/api/v1`\n  - `core/access`\n  - `store`\n  - `proto/api/v1`\n  - `internal/ratelimit`\n\nTreat the named revision and allowed paths as the complete source context. Do not rely on files outside those paths.\n\n## Independent policy\n\n> Any current member of a space may remove a different member from that space; self-removal is also allowed.\n\nThe policy is independent of the repository and is the conformance standard for this review.\n\n## Questions to answer\n\n1. In the actual implementation, may an ordinary space member remove a different member from that space?\n2. What visible authorization path applies when an administrator removes another member?\n3. What visible authorization path applies when a member removes themselves?\n4. Do the visible administrator-removal and self-removal paths differ? If so, explain exactly how.\n5. Does the implementation conform to the independent policy? Distinguish clearly among:\n   - removing another member as a space administrator;\n   - removing another member as an ordinary member; and\n   - leaving the space by removing oneself.\n\n## Required analysis\n\n- Trace `DeleteSpaceMember` from the API entry point through every authorization, role, identity, request-validation, and deletion condition visible within the allowed paths.\n- Identify the controlling source statements rather than inferring authorization solely from names, comments, RPC definitions, or expected product behavior.\n- Determine how the caller's identity, the target member's identity, and the caller's space role affect the outcome.\n- Examine any relevant access helpers, store operations, protobuf/API definitions, and rate-limit code within scope when they materially affect the result.\n- Separate authorization enforced by the handler or its visible callees from authorization that might hypothetically be supplied elsewhere.\n- Do **not** assume deployment middleware, interceptors outside the allowed paths, undocumented role grants, external gateway behavior, or other enforcement not demonstrated by the permitted source.\n- Do not treat absence of visible enforcement as proof that no out-of-scope enforcement exists. Instead, preserve that as a decisive missing fact where applicable and explain what conclusion can still be drawn from the in-scope code.\n- Preserve any other missing fact that prevents a definitive conclusion. State precisely what evidence is unavailable and why it matters.\n\n## Evidence and citations\n\nFor each material conclusion:\n\n- Cite the controlling repository file and relevant line number or line range at the specified revision.\n- Quote or closely describe the decisive condition or call.\n- Explain how that source controls the scenario in question.\n- If multiple files jointly determine behavior, cite each relevant source and describe the connection.\n- Keep the independent policy text distinct from repository evidence; do not imply that the policy originates in the codebase.\n\n## Conformance assessment\n\nCompare actual visible behavior against the policy exactly as supplied. Do not strengthen or weaken the policy. In particular:\n\n- The policy permits any current member to remove a **different** member, regardless of whether the caller is an administrator or an ordinary member.\n- The policy separately allows self-removal by a current member.\n- Do not infer that administrator status is required for removal of another member unless the permitted source demonstrates that requirement.\n- Do not treat a self-removal allowance as evidence for or against the separate permission for a member to remove someone else.\n\nClassify the result as **conforms**, **does not conform**, or **indeterminate from the permitted source**, and justify the classification scenario by scenario. If only part of the behavior is indeterminate, identify that part without obscuring conclusions supported by the source.\n\n## Output format\n\nProduce a concise source-based report with these sections:\n\n1. **Conclusion** — Direct answers to whether an ordinary member can remove another member and whether visible administrator and self-removal paths differ.\n2. **Observed control flow** — The relevant handler, checks, helpers, and deletion operation in execution order.\n3. **Scenario analysis** — Separate findings for administrator removing another member, ordinary member removing another member, and member removing themselves.\n4. **Policy comparison** — Scenario-by-scenario conformance analysis and an overall classification.\n5. **Decisive missing facts** — Any unavailable facts that materially limit the conclusion.\n6. **Source citations** — Precise file-and-line references for every material claim.\n\nReport only behavior supported by the specified revision and allowed paths. Do not propose fixes unless needed to clarify the nature of a policy mismatch, and do not invent implementation details."
